  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 338 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSoutheastern Wyoming and Western Nebraska will be under \nsouthwesterly flow aloft as a mid-level ridge begins to build across \nthe Central United States. With an embedded shortwave trough \npropagating across the region Wednesday afternoon combined with \nanother surge of monsoonal moisture (PWAT again ranging within the \n80th and 95th percentile), widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible, mainly south of I-80. However, no \nsevere weather is anticipated due to weak instability (MLCAPE ~100-\n200 J/Kg) thanks to poor mid-level lapse rates. Temperatures look to \nbe around or slightly warmer than average, in the low to mid-70s \neast of the Laramie Range and mid to upper 60s to the west. A weak \ncold front is progged to move through SE Wyoming and the W Nebraska \nPanhandle overnight Wednesday into Thursday, which may serve as a \nfocal point for more shower development into the evening hours. \n\nOn Thursday, an additional shortwave trough within the longwave \nridge pattern may result in another round of afternoon and evening \nshowers and thunderstorms, with slightly cooler daytime high \ntemperatures behind the front. With an effective bulk wind \ndifference of only 15-20 knots along with relatively weak \ninstability (MLCAPE < 500 J/Kg), no organized or severe weather is \nexpected, despite a post-frontal air mass characterized by dewpoints \nin the low to mid-50s across the Panhandle and PWAT exceeding\nthe 90th percentile yet again. This is rather rare for late \nSeptember, but we will certainly take any moisture we can get \ndespite nothing widespread expected...\n\nThe flow regime becomes more zonal on Friday which turns off our \nsupply for the anomalously rich monsoonal moisture seen the previous \ndays (PWAT ~0.9\"). This will decrease our chances for any meaningful \nprecipitation and will also be the start of a warming and drying \nperiod into the weekend. We will have to monitor increasing fire \nweather concerns into the weekend as long range ensemble guidance \nsuggests gusty, westerly winds mainly across SE Wyoming, combined \nwith daytime RH values of 20-25%. Not particularly concerned at this \ntime as we are still short of critical fire weather thresholds, but \nwill have to be monitored as we get closer given plenty of dry fuel \nand further warming underneath the building ridge. High temperatures \nthis weekend look to be 5-10 degrees above average, perhaps reaching \n80 degrees in areas east of the I-25 corridor.",
